[17592186200201 #edb/ref 1080 "images/bf/bf7cde9088d0559ea2732883b38763691f42c706a4f68b51bd0db14d3cfcf711.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200201 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200201 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "87a5ae30-ae4a-5f92-85a7-a2d709a9af1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200202 #edb/ref 1080 "images/d6/d66cabe448b2b33804721dd3cfa884de858a5e4fa6056991f612465070d54da5.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200202 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200202 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "94e867fb-4620-5583-b02d-b1726ccc7bac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200203 #edb/ref 1080 "images/d5/d5e67fe868b8abe416b3e41664baac5f84c84035615ee5dd4d9d010a6ec44132.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200203 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200203 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b33ae6a6-a514-5803-b56d-5904be3bbbc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200204 #edb/ref 1080 "images/85/85ca1d890803d6a8e3a264f99e20139bbac1217d7009280db5f01f8d8d18503a.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200204 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200204 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "223352b6-e4f2-5d15-8550-bc8a14fcd924" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200205 #edb/ref 1080 "images/c8/c8959920a0b91ec210049df92127769c80ebd17a1614df02480c3b84473f41db.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200205 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200205 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5569f83c-8129-5e08-9ea4-193d0af16b9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203684 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14613" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203684 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A = \\{ 4, 6, 12 \\}.$ Construct a graph for the following relation:\n\n$$\nR = \\{ (x,y) \\in A^2 \\::\\: x \\mid y\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203684 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53571345-258b-54e1-af9c-fa68d0d0f40e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203684 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in A^2$ with $x \\mid y.$\n\n- If $x = 4,$ then the values $y = 4, 12$ satisfy $x \\mid y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(4,4)$ and $(4,12),$ and we should plot these points on the graph.\n\n- If $x = 6,$ then the values $y = 6, 12$ satisfy $x \\mid y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(6,6)$ and $(6,12),$ and we should plot these points on the graph.\n\n- If $x = 12,$ then the value $y = 12$ satisfies $x \\mid y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(12,12),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/48/48836a0157b46f3f7af3ceb0db094612794b7da6c2d749bd8769f87da410b0d6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203685 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14614" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203685 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9d/9d07b5e999e4f0c9f403bf5ac3d61dfd12e1accd11a72d68a2f3b39196a58060.png)\n\n \n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{ 1, 3, 5 \\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(1, 5)$\n\nII. $(5, 5)$\n\nIII. $(3, 1)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203685 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c1451cc-7b5c-5f8f-9dfb-60c18319bce8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203685 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(1, 5)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has an arrow that starts at the node $1$ and ends at the node $5.$\n\n- The pair $(5, 5)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a loop from the node $5$ to itself.\n\n- The pair $(3, 1)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $3$ and ends at the node $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203698 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14650" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203698 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we have the sets $A$ and $B,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,3 \\}, \\qquad B=\\{-1,2,5\\}.\n$$\n\nConstruct a mapping diagram for the following relation:\n\n$$\nR= \\big\\{ (x,y) \\in A \\times B \\: : \\: x\\cdot y \\geq 4 \\big\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203698 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9306fe02-b1dd-584c-852f-27778aed9d8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203698 #edb/ref 1074 "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nWe're given the following sets:\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,3 \\}, \\qquad B=\\{-1,2,5\\}.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in A \\times B$ with $x\\cdot y \\geq 4.$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=5$ satisfies $x\\cdot y \\geq 4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,5),$ and we should add the arrow $1 \\rightarrow 5$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=2$ and $y=5$ satisfy $x\\cdot y \\geq 4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,2)$ and $(2,5),$ and we should add the arrows $2 \\rightarrow 2$ and $2 \\rightarrow 5$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the values $y=2$ and $y=5$ satisfy $x\\cdot y \\geq 4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(3,2)$ and $(3,5),$ and we should add the arrows $3 \\rightarrow 2$ and $3 \\rightarrow 5$ to the mapping diagram.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/7d/7ddf9a6fb23a475e75128b3c4bce145b5488eb5c12dfe592578cdf10435dc1a3.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203918 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203918 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203918 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203918 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203918 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203918 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203918 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203918 #edb/ref 1039 "Representing Binary Relations Using Mapping Diagrams" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203918 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186203698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203918 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "08140eb8-479d-5827-9dee-d51befd7aadd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203919 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203919 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203919 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203919 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203919 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203919 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203919 #edb/ref 1039 "Representing Binary Relations Using Graphs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203919 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186203684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203919 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "220647d1-71b8-5d0a-b497-e5d3ad7b6da2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203920 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203920 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203920 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203920 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203920 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203920 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203920 #edb/ref 1039 "Representing Binary Relations Using Digraphs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203920 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186203685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203920 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "65e9c4fd-dae7-5858-b008-ca0e144fa64a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204120 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204120 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204120 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204120 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204120 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204120 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204120 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204120 #edb/ref 1045 "Graphical Representations of Relations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204120 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186204690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204120 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204120 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204120 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b4fb5865-3b9a-5c6d-bd3e-b4ce289f995d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204228 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204228 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212112" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204228 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204228 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/01/015cb5b350ad2692c1371d20c367e6c01a81b981351ba33b61e6c95854862d63.png)\n\nThe plot of the binary relation $S,$ defined over the sets $X=\\{2,3,4\\}$ and $Y=\\{♣,♠\\},$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(2,♠)$\n\nII. $(3,♣)$\n\nIII. $(4,♣)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204228 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e5c1c70-9337-53c9-94bd-f7f57b8f63c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204228 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,♠)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(3,♣)$ does not belong to the relation since the plot does not contain a point with such coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(4,♣)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204230 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204230 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212120" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204230 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204230 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/84/847bcee3b9f9fc6672d5858111866141e043e390605cd92fb5579fd5193d7df4.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{1,2,3\\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(2,1)$\n\nII. $(3,3)$\n\nIII. $(2,3)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204230 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc05772e-ea0c-5a4b-9ddc-8e7c8974bf6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204230 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,1)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has an arrow that starts at the node $2$ and ends at the node $1.$\n\n- The pair $(3,3)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a loop from the node $3$ to itself.\n\n- The pair $(2,3)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $2$ and ends at the node $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204249 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204249 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213011" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204249 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204249 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we have the sets $A$ and $B,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{0.5,2,\\sqrt{5}\\},\\,B=\\{-1,0,1\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following mapping diagrams represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,{x}^{y}\\in Z\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204249 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59914551-baaa-5606-8583-0693c5e13bd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204249 #edb/ref 1074 "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nWe're given the following sets:\n\n$$\nA=\\{0.5,2,\\sqrt{5}\\},\\,B=\\{-1,0,1\\}.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with ${x}^{y}\\in Z.$\n\n- If $x=0.5,$ then the values $y=-1,0$ satisfy ${x}^{y}\\in Z.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(0.5,-1)$ and $(0.5,0),$ and we should add the arrows $0.5\\to -1$ and $0.5\\to 0$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=0,1$ satisfy ${x}^{y}\\in Z.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,0)$ and $(2,1),$ and we should add the arrows $2\\to 0$ and $2\\to 1$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=\\sqrt{5},$ then the value $y=0$ satisfies ${x}^{y}\\in Z.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(\\sqrt{5},0),$ and we should add the arrow $\\sqrt{5}\\to 0$ to the mapping diagram.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/d6/d66cabe448b2b33804721dd3cfa884de858a5e4fa6056991f612465070d54da5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204690 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204690 #edb/ref 1061 156613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204690 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fe03693e-6006-5f8a-85d0-c47be07217d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204690 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204691 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204691 #edb/ref 1061 156614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204691 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8b30856e-ec75-50dc-a46d-44fced53e002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204691 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204692 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204692 #edb/ref 1061 156617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204692 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13d13119-2421-512a-96c3-705a3abe3474" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204692 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204693 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204693 #edb/ref 1061 156618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204693 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7ceb273c-0209-5e50-bb43-184978f7b964" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204693 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204694 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204694 #edb/ref 1061 156622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204694 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f636e4f9-1c77-5713-ac0b-f2e7b50495da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204694 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204695 #edb/ref 1061 156623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204695 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a81964ff-60dc-5eb9-b608-34bfdd5a731d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204695 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205232 #edb/ref 1064 "Binary relations can be visualized using a **plot** (or **graph**). \n\nFor example, consider the binary relation $R$ defined over $A = \\{ 1, 2, 3 \\}$ and $B = \\{ 4, 5 \\},$ given by\n\n$$\nR = \\{ (1,4), (1,5), (2, 4), (3,5) \\},\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ corresponds to the plot shown below, where \n\n- the elements from the first set are written along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the elements from the second set are written along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)\\in R$ defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\n![](images/ff/ff049d0c79f75a14721d02576a83b945e9cfa1485353ad30a6c77d3ac156f3e4.png)\n\n \n\nPlots usually work well for finite and infinite (discrete or continuous) relations.\n\n**Watch out!** A relation may have points that line on the same vertical line, like the points $(1,4)$ and $(1,5)$ in $R.$ In general, a relation is *not* a function. Functions are a special case of relations, where the points with the same first component but different second components are not allowed. We'll learn more about these special kinds of relations in future lessons." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205232 #edb/ref 1065 9047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205232 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ab28a187-5e84-5bb2-994b-680c5df7d983" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205232 #edb/ref 1067 "Graphs of Relations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205238 #edb/ref 1064 "We've already seen how adjacency matrices describe finite relations. In this lesson, we'll learn other common ways to describe relations visually. \n\nSimilar to functions, binary relations can be visualized using **mapping diagrams**. \n\nFor example, consider the binary relation \n\n$$\nR = \\{ (1,4), (1,5), (2, 4), (3,5) \\},\n$$\n\ndefined on $A = \\{ 1, 2, 3 \\}$ and $B = \\{ 4, 5 \\}.$ \n\nThis relation corresponds to the diagram shown below, where the ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram.\nEach arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\n![](images/0e/0e5e4e1a1fe57428c551263959956ad0139766d5607b4afa6d918a2f121fe629.png)\n\n \n\nThe mapping diagram representation is usually used only for finite binary relations with relatively small underlying sets." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205238 #edb/ref 1065 9074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205238 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f3131b0a-f923-5d84-a190-128c95e45a0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205238 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205239 #edb/ref 1064 "Any binary relation that's defined over one set can be visualized using a **directed graph** (or **digraph**), where\n\n- the **nodes** ( or **vertices**) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an **arrow** from node $x$ to node $y$, and\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a **loop** from node $x$ to itself.\n\nFor example, consider the binary relation on $A = \\{ 1, 2, 3 \\}$ defined as \n\n$$\nR = \\{ (1,2), (1,3), (2,3), (3,3) \\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw a digraph corresponding to this relation. \n\nWe start by drawing three *nodes* corresponding to the elements of $A.$\n\n![](images/58/58e85e2eeb4a3d0382687b0d551379492504fb45ecdd93a4cb6885799a0f799d.png)\n\n \n\nThe locations of the nodes can be arbitrary. However, we should ensure that the diagram is easy to read.\n\nWe now go through the elements of $R$ to decide where to place *arrows* and *loops*:\n\n- $(1,2)\\in R,$ so we add the arrow $1 \\to 2$ to the digraph\n\n- $(1,3)\\in R,$ so we add the arrow $1 \\to 3$ to the digraph\n\n- $(2,3)\\in R,$ so we add the arrow $2 \\to 3$ to the digraph\n\n- $(3,3)\\in R,$ so we add the loop $3 \\to 3$ to the digraph\n\nTherefore, we have the following digraph:\n\n![](images/34/34297fa91d8eab0e076e3b4e22359189b64271741cf2624e8535921fb6f8b302.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205239 #edb/ref 1065 9075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205239 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "177ebb26-050a-5d21-8e09-6eb5696d6240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205239 #edb/ref 1067 "Directed Graphs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415984 #edb/ref 1080 "b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415984 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415984 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "039c3002-4123-5354-a54b-3c284e8cbe22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415985 #edb/ref 1080 "images/60/6048ff5e6150a98c1074716b6d58905171a9d074859b071b43d4795a841bca84.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415985 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415985 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05f6ce38-4ec7-5c4b-9a30-15cd62314ac4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415986 #edb/ref 1080 "images/d1/d1afa34c5eb553358d3c267cc5a38ed05d8e8566af2d650db714d0d5a9cdcb10.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415986 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415986 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0aca0654-5a0b-5d1a-9c1a-4a631fc7caaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415987 #edb/ref 1080 "images/5e/5eb68dca550b17d13a752b44fcf6c026f58e266100210d31834a73b19f9ba6e9.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415987 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415987 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c6d623c-ebdd-5e72-8f57-388e3aa09b2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415988 #edb/ref 1080 "c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415988 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415988 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0db9b435-693c-51f8-83dd-ff1f9738f23d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415989 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415989 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415989 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e527545-c8a4-5706-a526-a45e5bdba53b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415990 #edb/ref 1080 "images/f8/f8abac994a863447da2182e86df34cbdbcf019d58c9a606f507403f15890947d.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415990 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 175921860454